# Intent: The banner (N01) kept more LVGL objects alive than it showed. Its first build made the row's five objects, the strip container and all five glyphs (about 24 objects) whenever ANY strip glyph was on, and kept them 20 s after the last message; one always-on glyph (microphone live, Wi-Fi) therefore held about 3.4 KB of internal RAM at idle. Now each part exists only while it shows: the row's objects while a message is up and 2 s after it fades, the strip container only while at least one glyph is on, and each glyph's own objects (with the camera, battery and Wi-Fi sub-objects) only while its flag is on. A glyph that turns off is deleted at once. Nothing visible changes; the row is kept behind the strip (lv_obj_move_background) when it is built later than the strip.
# Placement: unit (the banner is drawn by the robot)
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/maipai_banner.cc (ours, patch N01)
# Check after a flash: with no message and the microphone glyph on, the memory line's internal_free is about 3 KB higher than on the N01 image; a message still fades in and out over the strip; the strip glyphs appear and disappear as the flags change.

diff --git a/firmware/main/hal/maipai_banner.cc b/firmware/main/hal/maipai_banner.cc
--- a/firmware/main/hal/maipai_banner.cc
+++ b/firmware/main/hal/maipai_banner.cc
@@ -5,8 +5,10 @@
  * (y 0 to 28). Its content is a holder with a leading icon and the text (a marquee only when the text overflows the text area,
  * or a dotted end for the session chip), and the strip of small glyphs at the right end. The row fades in and out at full
  * width; a short message never shrinks it and the session chip is a violet chip inside it. The face does not move for it.
- * Objects exist only while something shows (about 24) and are deleted after 20 s of nothing; idle there is a timer and one
- * 0.7 KB state in PSRAM.
+ * Objects exist only for what shows now: the row's five objects while a message is up (and 2 s after it fades), the strip's
+ * container while any glyph is on, and each glyph's own objects only while its flag is on (patch FI01: the first build kept the
+ * row, the container and all five glyphs alive whenever one glyph showed, about 3.4 KB of internal RAM). Idle there is a timer
+ * and one 0.7 KB state in PSRAM.
  */
